# Documentation Standards

Comments document the codebase for future readers. They should be concise, action-oriented, and on their own line (never inline).

## General Principles

- Comments document the codebase, not the developer (use chat for that)
- Comments always go on their own line, never inline
- Within method bodies, add brief `//` section headers before each logical block
- Method descriptions should be action-oriented, starting with verbs (e.g. "Verify the signature", "Compute the hash", "Read the request body")
- Every distinct logical operation (validation, computation, I/O, branching) should have a brief comment preceding it

## C# (XML Documentation)

For `*.cs` files:

- Use `/// <summary>` for types (classes, interfaces), public methods, and public properties
- Use `/// <param name="...">` for each method parameter
- Use `/// <returns>` for method return values
- Keep summary text concise and action-oriented; type summaries may use a few sentences when scope needs clarifying

## TypeScript (TSDoc)

For `*.ts` and `*.tsx` files, use [TSDoc](https://tsdoc.org):

- Use `/** ... */` doc comments for types, classes, and public methods
- The text before the first block tag is the summary; keep it brief and action-oriented
- Use `@param paramName - description` for each method parameter
- Use `@returns description` for method return values (omit for `void`)
- Within method bodies, add brief `//` section comments before each logical block (same as C#)
